.TH EXPR 1 "Riplix" "Ripple"
.SH NAME
expr \- evaluate an expression supplied as arguments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expr expression
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pass each operand and operator as a separate argument. Quote operators that
have a shell meaning, including *, parentheses, <, >, | and &.
.PP
Operators, from low to high precedence, are |, &, comparisons (<, <=, =, ==,
!=, >=, >), + and -, then *, / and %, then : for an anchored basic regular
expression. Parentheses group expressions. len gives a string's length;
unary ! and - are also available.
.PP
The colon operator returns the matched prefix length, or the first captured
subexpression when the pattern uses escaped parentheses. A failed match
returns zero or the empty string respectively. Arithmetic uses signed
32-bit integers; division by zero, overflow and malformed expressions fail.
.SH STATUS
Zero means a nonempty, nonzero result. One means zero or an empty result.
Two means an expression error; three reports an output or allocation error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
expr 12 + 3 '*' 4
expr hello : 'h.*'
expr abc123 : '[a-z]*\([0-9]*\)'
expr len Ripple
.fi
